{:up ["ALTER TABLE recipes ADD COLUMN reason TEXT"
      "ALTER TABLE recipes ADD COLUMN context TEXT"
      "ALTER TABLE recipe_history ADD COLUMN reason TEXT"
      "ALTER TABLE recipe_history ADD COLUMN context TEXT"
      "ALTER TABLE recipe_proposals ADD COLUMN reason TEXT"
      "ALTER TABLE recipe_proposals ADD COLUMN context TEXT"]
 ;; **Why a version exists and what its writer was doing**, in the agent's own
 ;; words, recorded at the moment it wrote: *evry create or change needs a reason,
 ;; that will be part of that verion and shown on the inbox when i look at the item
 ;; … and then also on that versions page.*
 ;;
 ;; **Two fields and not one, because they answer two questions.** *make it
 ;; explicit. lets make two fields there, both mandatory. reason and context.* The
 ;; first version of this migration had a single `reason` whose documentation asked
 ;; the agent to also say what it had been working on — which is a field with two
 ;; jobs and a docstring hoping for the second one. `reason` is why this change was
 ;; made; `context` is what the agent was doing when it made it, the session and the
 ;; task around the edit. A reviewer reading a rewrite months later needs both, and
 ;; only one of them can be guessed from the diff.
 ;;
 ;; **Three tables, and each is the same pair in the place that fact lives.**
 ;; `recipes` and `recipe_history` are 005's split kept whole: the current version is
 ;; the row and the superseded ones are history rows, so a per-version column has to
 ;; be on both or a Recipe's newest reason would vanish the moment it was superseded.
 ;; `recipe_proposals` is the third because a machine write does not always become a
 ;; version — on a Recipe the owner has touched, or a published one, it is filed as a
 ;; proposal instead — and *that* is the surface these were asked for on. They ride
 ;; with the proposal and are copied onto the version when the owner approves, so the
 ;; sentences he reads while deciding are the sentences the history keeps.
 ;;
 ;; **Nullable with no default, and NULL means 'not recorded' rather than 'none'** —
 ;; 005's call, made again for the same reason and with his blessing: *its ok when
 ;; old entries doent have that yet, but new ones must have it.* Every version and
 ;; every proposal that exists when this runs comes out NULL, and nothing can recover
 ;; what an agent was thinking months ago; a `DEFAULT ''` would make 'unrecorded' and
 ;; 'said nothing' the same answer, and the surfaces that show these would have to
 ;; guess which they were looking at. They show a line when there is one and stay
 ;; silent when there is not.
 ;;
 ;; **The requirement is on the writer, not on the column.** A machine `POST` or
 ;; `PUT` missing either one is a 400 before anything is written — see
 ;; `server.recipe-handler` — while the owner's own writes carry neither and never
 ;; will, because his edits are not the ones being reviewed. So the columns are
 ;; nullable in the schema and mandatory in the only path that can fill them, which
 ;; is the honest arrangement: `NOT NULL` here would have forced his every save to
 ;; invent a value, and a `CHECK` cannot tell a machine write from his.
 :down ["ALTER TABLE recipes DROP COLUMN reason"
        "ALTER TABLE recipes DROP COLUMN context"
        "ALTER TABLE recipe_history DROP COLUMN reason"
        "ALTER TABLE recipe_history DROP COLUMN context"
        "ALTER TABLE recipe_proposals DROP COLUMN reason"
        "ALTER TABLE recipe_proposals DROP COLUMN context"]}
